  "explain": {
   "text": "A fraction is one number, not two. Written as 3/4 it looks like a 3 and a 4 stuck together, but it names one place on the number line,[^1] and the two numerals tell you how to find it.\n\nRead the bottom first. It says how many equal pieces make one whole,[^2] so it names the piece: 4 means quarters. The top counts how many of those pieces you have,[^3] starting from zero, and where the count ends is the number.[^4] For 3/4, count three quarters: three pieces of the bar shade in and the mark stops at 0.75.\n\nThe two-numbers habit misleads. 5/8 has the bigger top and the bigger bottom, so it looks bigger than 3/4. Count it instead: five eighths is 0.625, short of 0.75.\n\nNor does a fraction have to stop before 1. Count 7 quarters: four fill the first bar, three more shade the second, and the mark lands at 1.75. That is 7/4, an [[improper fraction]] because its top is bigger than its bottom, and written as one whole and three quarters, 1 3/4, it is a [[mixed number]].[^5] Still one number.\n\nA fraction is always a fraction of something. Three quarters of an inch and three quarters of a metre are the same number of different wholes, so compare fractions only when they share a whole.[^6]\n\n**A fraction is one number: the bottom names the piece, the top counts the pieces from zero, and where the count ends is its place on the line.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
   "text": "A meter reading of 0.75 A is two digits but one current: you never read it as a 7 and a 5. A fraction works the same way. 3/4 says 'quarters, count three', and that is one place on the scale.",
   "breaks": "A decimal's pieces are fixed: tenths, then hundredths. A fraction picks its own piece with its bottom number, so one place can carry several fraction names, which a later lesson shows."
  },
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Steps**\n1. Read the bottom: that is the piece (8 means eighths).\n2. Count the top from zero, carrying on past 1 if it gets there.\n3. For a mixed number, start at the whole number and count the pieces on from it.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** top smaller than bottom, under 1; equal, exactly 1; bigger, over 1.\n\n**Worked example:** a drawing puts a hole at 1 3/8 inches. The pieces are eighths. Go to 1 and count three eighths on: 1 1/8, 1 2/8, 1 3/8. From zero that is 8 + 3 = 11 eighths, so 1 3/8 = 11/8 = 1.375 inches.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Reading 1 3/4 as 13/4. It is one and three quarters, 7/4.\n- Judging size by the top alone when the pieces differ.\n- Comparing fractions of different wholes."
  },
